Z||000004||Mach10||11-15-2001||09:49 PM||||Clutch... ||||reg||12
Z||000005||ITB 2M4||11-15-2001||11:38 PM||||Is it hard to get into first gear while the car is stationary?  If so, it could be the throw-out bearing.  A reccuring problem when I had my '87 Z-24 w/5spd.<BR>You might also check the nuts that hold the cables onto the shift brackets on the tranny.  If one is loose, you might not be getting the full travel out of the cable.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000006||jch82||11-16-2001||01:33 AM||||My 4speeds have a "feature" like that. If I shift into 3rd gear first then quickly into reverse, it won't grind....<P>Hope that helps!<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jch82_2.jpg"> ||||reg||12
Z||000007||tgowens||11-16-2001||02:41 AM||||another idea is a shifter cable going bad -- you didn't say what year/model/transmission you have but if it's a 5-speed Getrag I have seen a lot of cables that are simply stretched over time and makes it difficult to shift and it's usually reverse that is the hard one to shift into.....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ferrari_cdn||11-16-2001||02:49 AM||||It sounded like a problem I had.  On some occasions, (5% of the time) I can't shift into reverse.  Would only go halfway but get stuck.  When a friend told me that the idler gear wasn't lining up, so I just shift into first and then back in reverse it'll go in easily.  Try that?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000009||johnt671||11-16-2001||07:45 AM||||Shift into second gear for a second before going into reverse.  I would try bleeding the clutch a bit also.  Maybe a little air in there.||||reg||1
Z||000010||tesmith66||11-16-2001||08:59 AM||||Sounds like clutch trouble (imagine that!).<P>The clutch is not releasing all the way, causing the input shaft to turn, even with the clutch pedal pushed in. Since reverse is not synchronized, it refuses to go in while this is happening. Unfortunately, there are several things to check to get this sorted out. Also bear in mind that you will probably find more than one thing wrong...<P>Clutch pedal: The factory pedals are aluminum, and they can bend enough to cause this. You can't tell just by looking at it. It's easier to just replace it with a steel one. You will need to, anyway. They ALL bend and will continue to do so until replaced with a steel unit. I got mine from the Fiero Store for approx. $35.<P>Clutch Master Cylinder pushrod: It will bend over time. I have straightened mine with a hammer from under the dash (carefully!) before, but am cosidering making a steel one.<P>Clutch Master Cylinder: Yours is getting old...<P>Clutch Slave Cylinder: Quite possibly the most infuriating part on the Fiero. It is designed poorly, is VERY hard to bleed, is subjected to too much heat, and generally behaves badly. Archie has a great procedure for bleeding the clutch at <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com." TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com.</A> <P>Clutch Release Arm: If yours is cast iron, check for cracks. If yours is stamped steel, replace it with cast iron. I got mine at dealer.<P>The clutch itself could also be wearing out.<P>I know I gave you a lot here, and I apologize for the long post, but an entire book could be written on Fiero clutch problems alone. Just ask anyone on the forum, or search for "clutch" in the archives to see what I mean.<P>I was having a lot of trouble with mine, and I got fed up and replaced EVERYTHING from clutch pedal pad to clutch disk. I am now happy to report the system is working fine.<P>Good luck!<P>P.S. The Fiero Store has everything to do this, but you can get the clutch set, slave and master cylinders at the parts store.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe<p>[This message has been edited by tesmith66 (edited 11-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||troydeb||11-16-2001||09:07 AM||||Thanks for all the input guys. i dont know what transmission it is but its an '86 2m4. I think im gonna start at one end of it and check everything back, as it looks like anything good be the problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P><BR>Troy<P>'86 2m4||||reg||1
Z||000012||troydeb||11-16-2001||05:43 PM||||Well i think i fixed. Only driven it a few times since and it hasnt done it.  I cut the carpeting away from where the pedal hits the wall and it gave it another .25 of travel.  <BR>Hopefully its that simple <BR>haha<P>thanks for the help guys||||reg||1
